  "source": "src/lib/podcast/catalogue.snapshot.json — the 39 committed episode title and description values, and nothing else. Each evidence entry names the episode it came from and quotes that episode's own text; topics.test.ts checks every quote against THAT episode, not against the corpus as a whole.",
  "constraint": "Names are capped at 16 characters (TOPIC_NAME_MAX_LENGTH in src/lib/podcast/topics.ts). The ceiling is set by queries.test.ts's per-episode payload bound, not by taste, and is mirrored by rule.max(16) on studio/schemaTypes/topic.ts so an editor rename cannot quietly breach the budget the offline test measures. The vocabulary is proposed only from the corpus above, under the same no-outside-research rule that governs guest bios. Every topic carries at least four attributed episodes, so none is a facet of one — the evidence list is the support set itself rather than a claimed count. Deliberately excluded despite appearing in the corpus: \"Entrepreneurship\" and \"Founder\" — every guest is a founder and every episode is about entrepreneurship, so as facets they are definitionally true of all 39 and discriminate nothing.",
  "usageGuidanceForEnrichment": "For US-109 (Task 10), which assigns these to episodes. related.ts counts EVERY shared topic, so correlated labels double-weight relatedness. Two pairs are close enough to need a rule: (1) Wellness is the SECTOR (a wellness clinic, a wellness brand, financial wellness); Mental Health is the SUBJECT (burnout, therapy, meditation, emotional regulation). Assign both only when an episode genuinely does both. (2) Health Tech is technology applied to HEALTH outcomes (biowearables, VR therapeutics); AI & Technology is everything else technical (IoT, deep tech, AI tooling). Prefer the more specific label alone. Cap any single episode at six topics — the number studio/schemaTypes/episode.ts enforces and queries.test.ts's payload bound is measured against.",
